## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account dependency management, enforcement of security policies, and plan-driven gating in CI.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across accounts
- Cross-account data sources
- depends_on for orchestration
- Plan JSON validation
- CI gating for safety

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias = "sec"
  region = "us-east-1"
  profile = "security"
}

data "aws_kms_key" "policy_key" {
  provider = aws.sec
  key_id   = "alias/security/default"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  # ...
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm   = "aws:kms"
        kms_master_key_id = data.aws_kms_key.policy_key.key_id
      }
    }
  }
  depends_on = [data.aws_kms_key.policy_key]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle revoking access to the security key if a key rotation is required?
- How would you test this in a multi-region, multi-account rollout without impacting existing buckets?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Terraform governance requires strict isolation and policy enforcement when multiple teams/environments share global resources. This question probes your ability to combine per-environment workspaces with policy guardrails to prevent cross-environment impact.

## Key Concepts
- Per-environment Terraform Cloud workspaces for isolation
- Provider aliases to target the correct AWS account
- Sentinel policy to guard changes to shared VPC components
- CI validation: parse plan JSON and enforce env-tag constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sentinel-like illustrative policy (Terraform Cloud Sentinel syntax would differ)
policy "env_guard" {
  rule deny_shared_vpc_changes {
    // deny any change to shared VPC unless resource.tags.env == workspace.name
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tag drift across environments?
- How would you test policy changes locally before pushing to CI?
